<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darius III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/336-bce">336</a>–330 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Darius III was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. He was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> at the battles of Issus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> and was killed by his own officers in 330 BCE while fleeing eastward. His fall marked the end of the Achaemenid Empire and its absorption into Alexander's Macedonian realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-iii.jpg" alt="Darius III" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_III_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…was unusually rapid for an empire of more than two centuries: Alexander defeated Persian forces at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), occupied Persepolis and burned its palaces in 330 BCE, and <b>Darius III</b> was murdered by his own satrap Bessus that summer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus records that Persians and Saka broke the Athenian centre at the Battle of Marathon in 490 BCE and that Saka horsemen fought at Plataea in 479 BCE, and Saka cavalry served <b>Darius III</b> at the Battle of Gaugamela in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander of Macedon crossed into Asia in 334 BCE; within four years he had defeated the Persian armies in three great battles, burned the ceremonial capital at Persepolis, and seen <b>Darius III</b> murdered by his own officers.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>Bagoas, chief of the eunuchs under Artaxerxes III, became so powerful that he is said to have poisoned two kings and placed <b>Darius III</b> on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Great routed the far larger army of the Persian king <b>Darius III</b> near Gaugamela in northern Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-1">1 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They were horse-riding pastoralists famed as mounted archers, serving as cavalry for <b>Darius III</b> at Gaugamela and later for Alexander the Great and his successors.</blockquote><a href="/state/dahae">Dahae</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30,52&amp;year=-333" aria-label="Persis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5901 1068 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Darius III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6351 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6351 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30,52&amp;year=-333">What was here in 333 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alas! how great is the calamity of the Persians! Was it not enough that their king’s consort and sister was a prisoner in her lifetime, but she must, now she is dead also, be but meanly and obscurely buried?&#8221;</p><footer>On hearing of his wife's death in Alexander's camp (Plutarch, Life of Alexander) &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if our ruin be a debt that must be paid to the divine jealousy and the vicissitude of things, then I beseech you grant that no other man but Alexander may sit upon the throne of Cyrus.&#8221;</p><footer>His prayer after learning how Alexander had treated his family (Plutarch, Life of Alexander) &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; Plutarch's account, which he says most historians give &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
